WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.460
Signal masks help a program control when selected signals may interrupt sensitive work.

2
00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:14.840
They are not a complete safety system, but they are useful for narrowing interruption windows around setup, handler changes, state transitions, and shutdown coordination.

3
00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:18.300
A mask policy should be deliberate and documented.

4
00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:22.180
Initialization is a common place to use masks carefully.

5
00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:30.920
A program may need to establish handlers, create notification channels, initialize state used by the handler, and start worker threads or an event loop.

6
00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:35.260
If a signal arrives halfway through that sequence, the program needs a defined outcome.

7
00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:40.560
Blocking selected signals during setup can prevent the handler from observing incomplete state.

8
00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:44.200
Masks can also protect important transitions.

9
00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:53.820
For example, a program may switch from accepting work to shutting down, or from privileged setup to ordinary processing, or from one child-management state to another.

10
00:00:53.920 --> 00:01:01.840
During these transitions, an asynchronous request should not leave the program believing two incompatible states are true at the same time.

11
00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:04.920
Restoration matters as much as blocking.

12
00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:10.940
Code that blocks signals for a critical section should restore the previous mask predictably, including on error paths.

13
00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:18.160
A signal left blocked by accident can make shutdown, supervision, or child notifications unreliable.

14
00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:23.220
A signal unblocked too early can expose half-initialized state.

15
00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:29.580
In threaded programs, mask policy becomes more important because masks may differ by thread.

16
00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:40.960
Teams often choose a simple policy: block selected signals in worker threads and route handling through a known path, or define exactly which thread or loop observes signal requests.

17
00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:45.460
The best policy is the one that the team can explain, test, and maintain.
